  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Nine strikeouts and a shutout loss. This is what happens when the pitching gives you nothing to work with and the offense refuses to show.",
    "what_this_means": "The Cards got to two runs by the fourth and never looked back, while the Padres mustered a single hit across nine innings. That's not a competitive game. That's a statement of complete domination by St. Louis. Lucas Giolito lasted five innings and gave up three runs, which is survivable in a normal game. The bullpen did its part: Kyle Hart threw two scoreless innings and kept the door from opening wider. But none of it mattered. When your lineup combines for one hit and nine strikeouts, the pitching line becomes irrelevant. Wandy Peralta exited after one inning with no damage done, but there was nothing to protect. The offense didn't just fail to capitalize. It never materialized at all. They left two runners on base in the seventh, the only moment the game offered anything close to a chance, and did nothing with it. That's where this game broke: not in the moments of drama, but in the complete absence of one.",
    "what_to_watch": "The inconsistency is showing up again, and it's not subtle. A team that scored five runs yesterday managed none today against the same opponent. That's not variance. That's an alarm. They needed to stay close, and they didn't. The pitching was willing to pitch a close game, but the bats weren't willing to play one. Now they're eight games back with a division that doesn't wait for lineup flukes to pass. One hit through nine innings is a disaster, not a learning moment.",
